content.text:
Aight, listen up. **Dependency Injection in C#** is when your class stops runnin’ around the neighborhood buyin’ its own tools, and instead someone hands it what it needs at the door.

## The old way: class gets its own stuff

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly EmailSender _emailSender;

    public OrderService()
    {
        _emailSender = new EmailSender();
    }

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        // place order...
        _emailSender.SendEmail("Order placed!");
    }
}
```

This class is sayin’:

> “I don’t need nobody. I’ll make my own EmailSender.”

That sounds tough, but it’s bad business.

Why? Because `OrderService` is now **tightly coupled** to `EmailSender`. If you wanna swap email for SMS, mock it in tests, or change how messages are sent, now you gotta crack open `OrderService` and mess with the code.

That’s sloppy.

---

## The gangster way: someone else provides the dependency

First, you make an interface — the contract.

```csharp
public interface IMessageSender
{
    void SendMessage(string message);
}
```

Then an implementation:

```csharp
public class EmailSender : IMessageSender
{
    public void SendMessage(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Email sent: {message}");
    }
}
```

Now your service doesn’t care *who* sends the message. It just knows somebody better send it.

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly IMessageSender _messageSender;

    public OrderService(IMessageSender messageSender)
    {
        _messageSender = messageSender;
    }

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        // place order...
        _messageSender.SendMessage("Order placed!");
    }
}
```

Now `OrderService` says:

> “I don’t care if it’s email, SMS, carrier pigeon, whatever. Just give me someone who follows the IMessageSender rules.”

That’s Dependency Injection.

---

## Who does the injecting?

In modern C#, usually the **DI container** handles it.

In ASP.NET Core, you register your crew in `Program.cs`:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IMessageSender, EmailSender>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<OrderService>();
```

That means:

> “Yo container, whenever somebody asks for `IMessageSender`, send `EmailSender`.”

Then ASP.NET Core can build `OrderService` automatically:

```csharp
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly OrderService _orderService;

    public OrdersController(OrderService orderService)
    {
        _orderService = orderService;
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult PlaceOrder()
    {
        _orderService.PlaceOrder();
        return Ok();
    }
}
```

The controller doesn’t create `OrderService`.  
`OrderService` doesn’t create `EmailSender`.  
Everybody gets what they need from the boss: the DI container.

---

## The main idea

Dependency Injection means:

> **Don’t create your own dependencies. Have them handed to you.**

Instead of this:

```csharp
var sender = new EmailSender();
```

inside your class, you do this:

```csharp
public OrderService(IMessageSender sender)
{
    _sender = sender;
}
```

That makes your code:

- easier to test
- easier to change
- less tightly coupled
- cleaner
- more flexible

---

## Testing example

Without DI, testing is annoying because your class is stuck using the real `EmailSender`.

With DI, you can slide in a fake one:

```csharp
public class FakeMessageSender : IMessageSender
{
    public void SendMessage(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Fake send: {message}");
    }
}
```

Then:

```csharp
var fakeSender = new FakeMessageSender();
var service = new OrderService(fakeSender);

service.PlaceOrder();
```

Boom. No real email. No drama.

---

## In gangster terms

Dependency Injection is like this:

> Your class is a boss.  
> The boss don’t go fetchin’ tools.  
> The crew brings the tools.  
> And as long as the tool does the job, the boss don’t care who made it.

So `OrderService` doesn’t say:

> “I need Joey EmailSender specifically.”

It says:

> “I need someone who can send messages.”

That’s clean architecture, boss.

